lemma_76.statement:
(The exact obstruction to (GC): no Frobenius-stable Jordan block of size two, and the multiplicity count.) Let \ell be a prime, put R := \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t^{\pm 1}], let V be a nonzero finite-dimensional \mathbb{Q}_\ell-vector space, let T \in \mathrm{GL}(V), and regard V as an R-module by t\cdot v := T(v). For a monic irreducible f \in \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t] dividing \chi_T, let e_f := v_f(\chi_T) be the exponent of f in \chi_T and define the TOTAL multiplicity m_f := e_f \cdot \deg f = \sum_{\alpha:\,f(\alpha)=0} \mathrm{mult}_\alpha(\chi_{T_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell}}) \;(= \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f^{\infty}]); write V[f] := \ker f(T), V[f^2] := \ker f(T)^2 and V[f^{\infty}] := \bigcup_{N\ge1}\ker f(T)^N. Prove, from linear algebra over \mathbb{Q}_\ell only and without reproving lemma_60, lemma_68 or lemma_74:
(i) one always has \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f] \le m_f, and T is semisimple on V if and only if equality holds for every f \mid \chi_T; more precisely \dim_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V[f] = m_f if and only if T is semisimple on the f-primary piece V[f^{\infty}]. (Note: with m_f read as the exponent e_f the inequality is FALSE for deg f > 1 — e.g. V = \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t]/(f) with f irreducible quadratic and T = multiplication by t gives \dim V[f] = 2 > 1 — so the total-multiplicity normalisation above is essential; for deg f = 1, and hence over \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell, the two readings agree and (i) says that the number of Jordan blocks at a root is at most its multiplicity in \chi_T, with equality iff T is semisimple.)
(ii) T is semisimple if and only if V contains NO R-submodule isomorphic to R/(f^2) for any monic irreducible f \in \mathbb{Q}_\ell[t];
(iii) equivalently, T is NOT semisimple if and only if there exist a monic irreducible f \mid \chi_T and vectors x, y \in V with y \neq 0, f(T)y = 0 and f(T)x = y, i.e. a T-stable two-step chain inside one f-primary piece;
(iv) BLOCK COUNT: after extending scalars to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_\ell, f splits into distinct roots (characteristic zero), and \dim V[f^2] - \dim V[f] equals the total number of Jordan blocks of T of size \ge 2 belonging to the roots of f; in particular T is semisimple on the f-primary piece V[f^{\infty}] if and only if V[f^2] = V[f];
(v) GEOMETRIC PACKAGING: let (GC_2) be the statement that for every m \ge 1, every smooth projective geometrically connected Y over \mathbb{F}_{q^m}, and every n \ge 0, writing V := H^n_{\mathrm{et}}(Y_{\overline{\mathbb{F}}_q},\mathbb{Q}_\ell) and T := arithmetic Frobenius over \mathbb{F}_{q^m}, the equivalent conditions (i)-(iv) hold (with m_f the total multiplicity defined above). Prove that (GC_2) is equivalent to (GC), the hypothesis of lemma_59, and hence that (GC_2) implies the conjecture. State the geometric target explicitly as: there is no Frobenius-stable Jordan block of size >1 in any primary piece of any H^n.
